  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nPersistent upper high over the Southern Rockies will remain fixed\nover the region through at least the remainder of late July,\ndriving the weather pattern through the week ahead. GFS 500-mb\nheight anomalies suggest this high is 3-4 standard deviations\nstronger than is typical for the middle of the North America\nMonsoon. The pattern is not favorable for widespread rain, as any\nstorms that do form will be mostly driven by recycled moisture \nand convective temperatures/terrain forcing producing meager \ninstability. Precipitable water values are just over an inch \nthrough much of central New Mexico, with drier air across far west\nTexas. Scattered thunderstorms are expected once again across the\nsouthern NM high elevations and portions of the southwest New \nMexico lowlands. El Paso/Las Cruces should remain dry. Main threat\nwill be localized flash flooding, as storm motions remain very \nslow if not stationary and capable of 1-2 inch/hour rainfall \nrates.\n\nHot temperatures will also continue into the week ahead due to \nthis suffocating upper ridge. Highs will run 5-10 degrees above \nnormal for late July, which means lowland highs in the 100 to 105\ndegree range. Another Heat Advisory will need to be considered \nfor El Paso and Rio Grande Valley this week.\n\nMinimal changes in the strength or orientation of the high for \nTuesday onward will make it difficult to pinpoint any appreciable \nday-to- day changes with sensible weather elements. Overall \nweather pattern will continue to be warmer-than-normal \ntemperatures and isolated rain chances focused mainly around the \nsouthern NM high elevations. GFS/ECMWF both agree in a slight \nretrograde of the upper high as we head into the first week of \nAugust, which is still a poor weather pattern for rain chances.",
